WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:06.170
So how much energy has to be
given to food to heat it up?

00:00:06.170 --> 00:00:09.160
If you want to heat up an
egg by 10 degrees Celsius,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:12.410
how many joules does it take to do that?

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:14.530
So the answer to this, it
turns out, is contained

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:16.995
in a simple mathematical equation.

00:00:16.995 --> 00:00:19.120
A little later on, I'm
going to try to convince you

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.970
that if you know how to use it
properly, you have the opportunity

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:27.880
to cook a perfect egg without investing
in a rather expensive immersion heater.

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.495
That is, by understanding the
equations that we give you,

00:00:30.495 --> 00:00:34.670
you really will have the opportunity
to use them to invent new recipes,

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:36.200
if you so desire.

00:00:36.200 --> 00:00:38.980
Now having said that as introduction,
let me go into the equation.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:44.686
The equation says that Q equals mcp
delta T. So Q is the amount of heat

00:00:44.686 --> 00:00:46.060
that you've dumped into the food.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.740
And that is a quantity
that is measured in joules.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.140
m is the mass of the food.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:54.250
For the purposes of this equation,
m should be measured in kilograms.

00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:56.840
So delta T is the
temperature difference.

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:00.670
That's the final temperature of the
food minus the initial temperature

00:01:00.670 --> 00:01:01.240
of the food.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:05.590
And it should be measured in either
degrees Celsius or degree Kelvin.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:09.970
And finally, cp is a number that we
call the specific heat of a material.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:13.660
It is the number that characterizes
how much something heats up when

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:15.920
you apply a specific amount of heat.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:21.400
And it is measured in the units of
joules per kilogram per degree Celsius,

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:22.519
or degree Kelvin.

00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:24.310
And this is a number
that we will give you,

00:01:24.310 --> 00:01:27.070
and that actually is
different for different foods.

00:01:27.070 --> 00:01:29.560
So it turns out that the
specific heat for water

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:34.040
is 4.18 joules per gram degree Kelvin.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.070
That's the specific heat for water.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:39.230
Now water is an important cooking
material, and we will talk about it.

00:01:39.230 --> 00:01:41.820
So for eggs, another
important cooking material,

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:46.750
their specific heat is about 3.18
joules per gram per degree Kelvin.

00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.440
For milk, it's about 3.85.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:50.920
It's closer to water.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.020
For beef, depending on the cut of beef,
it's somewhere between 2 and 1/2 and 3

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:58.220
and 1/2 joules per gram degree Kelvin.

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.942
For air, the specific heat of air is 1.

00:02:00.942 --> 00:02:02.650
The specific heat of
olive oil, something

00:02:02.650 --> 00:02:05.615
which is also useful for
cooking, is about 1.97 joules

00:02:05.615 --> 00:02:06.740
per gram per degree Kelvin.

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:10.090
So it's actually a lower specific
heat by about a factor of 2,

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:11.077
than that of water.

00:02:11.077 --> 00:02:12.910
Now actually those two
numbers, if you think

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:15.410
about them, the specific heat
of water and the specific heat

00:02:15.410 --> 00:02:18.640
of olive oil, the fact that most
foods are between those two numbers

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:21.670
is not an accident by any means.

00:02:21.670 --> 00:02:24.387
In fact, the main ingredient
of all food is water.

00:02:24.387 --> 00:02:27.220
This is something that we're going
to have to think about constantly

00:02:27.220 --> 00:02:28.178
throughout this course.

00:02:28.178 --> 00:02:31.370
It will come up again and again
in one context or another.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:33.790
And another important
ingredient to food of course,

00:02:33.790 --> 00:02:35.590
is fat, which olive oil is a fat.

00:02:35.590 --> 00:02:37.810
And fats tend to have a
specific heat around 2.

00:02:37.810 --> 00:02:40.560
And foods like beef, it's
somewhere between 2 to 4.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.040
And that's essentially why.

00:02:42.040 --> 00:02:43.748
It's a little more
complicated than that.

00:02:43.748 --> 00:02:46.080
But that's not a bad
explanation to get us started.

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.530
Now so to get us started,
let's actually do something.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:54.860
So I want to calculate the energy that
it takes to boil one cup of water.

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:59.530
So it turns out that one
cup of water is 237 grams.

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:03.410
And so what I want to do is start
with water that's at room temperature.

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:06.760
So the temperature in this room
is about 23 degrees Celsius.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:10.270
And I'd like to heat it to the boiling
point, which is 100 degrees Celsius.

00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.469
And I'd like to know how much
energy do I have to dump in.

00:03:13.469 --> 00:03:16.510
And when you multiply this out, it
turns out that the number that you get

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:20.720
is 76,280 joules.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.172
So that's how much energy it takes to
boil a cup of water, 76,280 joules.

00:03:26.172 --> 00:03:28.630
Now it's a little bit hard to
know how to think about that,

00:03:28.630 --> 00:03:30.171
because it's just a number in joules.

00:03:30.171 --> 00:03:32.515
So one way to think
about it, how much wood

00:03:32.515 --> 00:03:34.300
would it take to make this much energy?

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.758
Now if you take the energy
density of wood, which I already

00:03:36.758 --> 00:03:39.430
told you is 14 megajoules
per kilogram, then you

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:43.840
can calculate for yourself that
the amount of wood that it takes

00:03:43.840 --> 00:03:49.780
is 76,280 joules divided by
14 megajoules per kilogram.

00:03:49.780 --> 00:03:56.760
We find that it takes 5.4 grams of wood,
in order to create this much energy.

